  {
   "id": "bekhorot.37b.8",
   "daf": "37b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "נִקְּבָה מְלֹא וְכוּ׳. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: כַּמָּה נְקִיבַת הָאוֹזֶן? מְלֹא כַרְשִׁינָה. רַבִּי יוֹסֵי בְּרַבִּי יְהוּדָה אוֹמֵר: בְּכַעֲדָשָׁה. וְאֵיזוֹ הִיא יְבֵשָׁה? שֶׁאִם תִּינָּקֵב וְאֵינָהּ מוֹצִיאָה טִיפַּת דָּם. רַבִּי יוֹסֵי בֶּן הַמְשׁוּלָּם אוֹמֵר: יְבֵשָׁה כְּדֵי שֶׁתְּהֵא נִפְרֶכֶת.",
   "en": "§ The mishna stated that if the ear of a firstborn animal «was pierced» with a hole «the size of» a bitter vetch, the animal is slaughtered because of it. With regard to this, «the Sages taught» in a baraita: «How large» must «the piercing of the ear» be? It must be «the size of a karshina. Rabbi Yosei, son of Rabbi Yehuda, says: Like» the size of «a lentil,» which is slightly smaller. «And what is a desiccated» ear that is considered a blemish? It is an ear «that if it is pierced, it does not discharge a drop of blood. Rabbi Yosei ben HaMeshullam says: Desiccated» means that the ear is «so» dry «that it will crumble» if one touches it."
  }
 ],
